readings.c1s1:
{{smaller block|''Yellings'' loud and deep. {{right|''Drayton.''}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Pale spectres grin around me,
And stun me with the ''yellings'' of damnation.</poem>
{{right|''Johnson.''}}}}

'''yelloch''' (yel′oċh), ''v. i.'' [A var. of ''yell''<sup>1</sup>, with a guttural termination.] To scream; yell; shriek. [Scotch.]
{{smaller block|But an auld useless carline . . . flung herself right in my sister’s gate, and ''yelloched'' and skirled, that you would have thought her a whole generation of hounds.
{{right|''Scott'', Pirate, xxx.}}}}

'''yelloch''' (yel′oċh), ''n.'' [< ''yelloch'', ''v.''] A shrill cry; a yell. [Scotch.]

'''yellow''' (yel′ō), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [Also dial. ''yullow'', ''yallow'', ''yaller'', etc.; < ME. ''yelow'', ''yelowe'', ''yelwe'', ''ȝelwe'', ''ȝelowe'', ''yolwe'', ''ȝelu'', etc., also ''ȝalow'', ''yalu'', etc., < AS. ''geolu'', ''geolo'' (''geolw-'') = OS. ''gelo'' = MD. ''ghelu'', D. ''geel'' = OHG. ''gelo'' (''gelw-''), MHG. ''gel'' (''gelw-''), G. ''gelb'' = Icel. ''gulr'' = Sw. Dan. ''gul'', yellow, = L. ''helvus'', light-yellow; akin to Gr. χλόη, verdure, χλωρός, yellowish-green, OBulg. ''zelenŭ'', yellow, green, Lith. ''zalias'', green, Skt. ''hari'', yellow: see ''chlor-'', ''gold''. Perhaps also akin to Gr. χολή = L. ''fel'', bile, gall, = E. ''gall'': see ''gall''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''a.'' Of a color resembling that of gold, butter, etc. See II. {{smaller|''Yellow'' is sometimes used in the sense of ‘jaundiced,’ ‘jealous,’ etc., the color being regarded as a token or symbol of jealousy, envy, melancholy, etc.: a usage no doubt connected with the figurative notions attaching to jaundice, the skin having a yellow hue in that disease.}}
{{smaller block|His Nekke is ''ȝalowe'', aftre colour of an Orielle, that is a Ston well schynynge. {{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 48.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>His here, that was ''yalu'' and bright,
Blac it bicome anonright.</poem>}}

readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Gy of Warwike'', p. 220. (''Halliwell.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>She gave it Cassio, but thereat
Why roll your ''yellow'' eye?</poem>
{{right|''Tragedie of Othello the Moor'', quoted in Furness’s [Variorum Othello, p. 398 (App.).}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>A primrose by a river’s brim
A ''yellow'' primrose was to him,
And it was nothing more.</poem>
{{right|''Wordsworth'', Peter Bell, i. 12.}}}}

'''Acute yellow atrophy of the liver''', {{smaller|a disease characterized by a granular fatty degeneration of various tissues of the body, particularly of the glands and muscles, the changes being usually most evident in the liver.}}—'''Blue-winged yellow warbler.''' {{smaller|See ''warbler.''}}—'''Imperial yellow porcelain.''' {{smaller|See ''imperial.''}}—'''King’s yellow worm.''' {{smaller|See ''redia.''}}—'''Order of the Yellow String.''' {{smaller|See ''order.''}}—'''Spotted yellow flycatcher†.''' {{smaller|Same as ''African warbler.'' See ''warbler.''}}—'''Spotted yellow warbler.''' {{smaller|See ''warbler'', and cut under ''spotted.''}}—'''To wear yellow hose''' or '''stockings†''', {{smaller|to be jealous.}}

{{smaller block|Jealous men are either knaves or coxcombs; be you neither; you ''wear yellow hose'' without cause.
{{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Northward Ho, i. 3.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
¶
'''Yellow adder’s-tongue, admiral, antimony.''' See the nouns.—'''Yellow ant,''' a species of ant, ''Lasius flavus,'' common to Europe and North America.—'''Yellow arsenic†.''' See ''arsenic'', 1.—'''Yellow ash, asphodel, avens.''' See the nouns.—'''Yellow baboon,''' the wood-baboon.—'''Yellow bachelor’s-buttons.''' See ''bachelor’s buttons.''—'''Yellow balsam.''' (''a'') The touch-me-not, ''Impatiens Noli-tangere.'' (''b'') See ''balsam.''—'''Yellow bark.''' Same as ''Bolivian bark'' (which see, under ''bark''<sup>2</sup>).—'''Yellow bass,''' the brass-bass.—'''Yellow bear,''' the larva of a common bombycid moth, ''Spilosoma virginica,'' commonly called the ''Virginia tiger-moth.'' [U. S.]—'''Yellow bedstraw.''' See ''bedstraw'', 2 (''a'').—'''Yellow belle,''' a rare British geometrid moth, ''Apsilates citraria.''—'''Yellow berries.''' Same as ''Persian berries'' (which see, under ''Persian'').—'''Yellow birch.''' See ''birch.''—'''Yellow bird’s-nest,''' ''Hypopitys multiflora'' (''Monotropa Hypopitys''). See ''bird’s-nest'', 1 (''b'').—'''Yellow boa,''' the yellow snake (see below).—'''Yellow box,''' ''Eucalyptus melliodora,'' of New South Wales and Victoria, a large tree with a thick trunk and spreading top. The wood is prized for various kinds of artizans’ work, for ship-building, fuel, etc. The name is also ascribed to the bloodwood, ''E. corymbosa,'' of New South Wales and Queensland, of which the wood is very hard when dry, and durable underground.—'''Yellow boy.''' (''a'') A gold coin. [Slang.]
{{smaller block|John did not starve his cause: there wanted not ''yellow-boys'' to fee counsel. {{right|''Arbuthnot'', Hist. John Bull, i. 6.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|<poem>The cercles of his eyen in his heed
They gloweden bitwixe ''yelow'' and reed.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Knight’s Tale, l. 1274.}}}}
{{smaller block|Your French-crown-colour beard, your perfect ''yellow.''
{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., i. 2. 98.}}}}
'''2.''' The yolk of an egg; the vitellus: opposed to the ''white'', or the surrounding albumen.—'''3.''' ''pl.'' Jaundice, especially jaundice in cattle (see ''jaundice''); hence, figuratively, jealousy.
{{smaller block|His horse, . . . sped with spavins, rayed with the ''yellows.''
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., iii. 2. 54.}}}}
{{smaller block|Thy blood is yet uncorrupted, ''yellows'' has not tainted it.
{{right|''Two Lancashire Lovers'' (1640), p. 27. (''Halliwell.'')}}}}
'''4.''' ''pl.'' Dyer’s-weed. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]—'''5.''' Same as ''peach-yellows.''
{{smaller block|The ''yellows'' is its [the peach’s] most fatal disease.
{{right|''New Amer. Farm Book'', p. 232.}}}}
